  "body": "The native wire EDM job files post accepts a convex prismatic punch with straight oblique faces. Before this change, it accepted only an axis-aligned rectangular punch.\n\n## Port onto main (2026-11-23)\n\nThis branch is now merged with `main` (#2984 and #3537 included). The port keeps the writer and the setup of `main` as the frame. It adds the checks of this PR into that frame.\n\nWhat stays from `main`, unchanged:\n- `prepare()` builds the guarded zero-step `PunchSetup20` first. The stock span, the 20 mm stock, the micrometre start hole and the hole-in-stock checks run before the contour checks.\n- `export_with_review` refuses a separation length that is not 1.0 mm.\n- The ISO, SBR and SBL files go through `crates/post/src/agie_setup.rs`. The old `jescript.rs` writer of this branch is not used.\n- The rectangular output is byte for byte the same. The tests of `main` for the rectangle pass without a change.\n\nWhat this PR adds to `main`:\n- `check_punch_envelope` replaces the rectangle refusal. It refuses a reentrant corner. It checks the 0.4 mm stock margin at each vertex. It checks the miter point of the 0.245 mm rough offset and the wire allowance at each corner.\n- `entry_face` and `outside_seam` replace the old entry search. The entry can be on an oblique face. The 3 mm lead and the 3 mm face length on each side are measured along the face, in exact integer arithmetic. When the perpendicular foot falls between 0.00001 mm grid points, the post selects an exterior grid point within 0.00001 mm. It then checks the envelope again on the emitted lines.\n- Refusal texts name the problem, not the machine (#3537).\n- The examples `examples/agie/oblique-punch.camscript` and `slope-punch.camscript` use the post name \"Native wire EDM job files\".\n- The `camcraft-mcp` skill row says \"a convex straight-sided punch\" in place of \"rectangular punch\".\n\nProcess change: yes\n\n## Pinning tests (`camcraft-post` lib, `agie::tests`)\n- `oblique_punch_preserves_nominal_vertices_and_perpendicular_seam`: the nominal path of the 3:4 pentagon, both windings, and the 3 mm face-length limit.\n- `oblique_punch_posts_the_zero_step_setup_and_its_faces`: the full ISO file of the oblique punch through `export_with_review`, the SBL fields of the zero-step setup, and the refusal of separation 0.0 with no output directory.\n- `oblique_seam_rounds_to_the_exterior_without_moving_nominal_vertices`: the exterior grid seam.\n- `outward_rounding_cannot_consume_the_required_lead_clearance`: the grid refusal.\n- `an_acute_miter_needs_more_stock_than_a_round_corner`: the corner-clearance refusal and the convex refusal.\n- `every_face_keeps_the_straight_stock_margin`: the 0.4 mm face margin of `main` stays.\n- `separation_needs_three_millimetres_on_each_side_of_the_entry`: the entry face-length refusal of `main`.\n\n## Readings\nHead `9787bf79e`. It merges cleanly with `main` at `09ad779ee`.\n- `cargo xtask gate quick --base origin/main`: 11 steps, 0 failed. `camcraft-post` lib: 103 passed. Nine `camcraft-app` suites (the example catalog and first-part suites): 69 passed, 4 ignored (heavy).\n- By name: `agie_native` 11 passed, `mcp_wire_contour` 8 passed, `mcp_doc_drift` 6 passed.\n- `cargo clippy -p camcraft-post --all-targets -- -D warnings`: clean.\n- CLI: `examples/agie/oblique-punch.camscript` wrote `POLY20` (entry X-12.50000 Y0.00000, all five nominal vertices). `slope-punch.camscript` wrote `SLOPE20` (exterior entry X-12.64765 Y0.50336). The receipt of each says `\"working_steps_generated\": false`.\n- Heavy lanes: `gate heavy --dry-run` reaches 61 `camcraft-app` heavy steps through the `post` dependency. This is a `P6` PR, so no heavy step is required (\"Heavy lanes at review\"). None ran.\n- Light lane: `gate light --dry-run` plans 4 steps (`motion` lib, `adaptive_troch_parallel`, `app` `pocket_pass_start`). None of them is in the changed code. They did not run, because the local disks had no space for a release build. The review must run them.\n\n## Not exercised\n- No fresh offline control import of the ported writer. The earlier control plots (POLY20, SLOPE20, four passes) used the old writer of this branch. A new import must show the oblique path and zero working steps.\n- No physical cut.\n\nEffort: high\nChangelog: The native wire EDM job files post accepts convex prismatic punches with straight oblique sides. It checks the corner and separation clearances before it writes the job.\n\n🤖 Generated with [Claude Code](https://claude.com/claude-code)\n\nFixes #2953\n",
